h1={'id':'lem-surface-completion-base-change-preserves-closed-fibre-local-completions','kind':'lemma','title':'Completion base change preserves completed local rings on the closed fibre','deps':['def-axiom-of-choice','thm-completion-of-a-noetherian-local-ring','cor-completion-commutes-with-finite-quotients-and-submodules','thm-affine-fibre-product-tensor-ring'],'statement':r'''Assume AC. Let $(A,\mathfrak m)$ be a Noetherian local ring, $\widehat A$ its maximal-adic completion, and $X$ a scheme locally of finite type over $A$. Put $Y=X\times_{\operatorname{Spec}A}\operatorname{Spec}\widehat A$. The closed fibres of $X$ and $Y$ are canonically isomorphic. If $y\in Y$ lies on this fibre and $x$ is its corresponding point, then the local homomorphism $\mathcal O_{X,x}\to\mathcal O_{Y,y}$ induces an isomorphism of maximal-adic completions.''','strategy':r'''Work in $X=\operatorname{Spec}B$, so $Y=\operatorname{Spec}C$ with $C=B\otimes_A\widehat A$ by thm-affine-fibre-product-tensor-ring. The completion quotient supplier gives $C/\mathfrak m^nC\cong B/\mathfrak m^nB$ for every $n\ge1$, including the closed-fibre identification at $n=1$. Let $\mathfrak p\subset B$ and $\mathfrak q\subset C$ represent corresponding fibre points. They contain $\mathfrak mB$ and $\mathfrak mC$, respectively, and are the same prime modulo these ideals. Since $\mathfrak m^nB\subset\mathfrak p^n$ and $\mathfrak m^nC\subset\mathfrak q^n$, the preceding isomorphism identifies $B/\mathfrak p^n$ with $C/\mathfrak q^n$. Localize at the corresponding primes, giving $B_{\mathfrak p}/\mathfrak p^nB_{\mathfrak p}\cong C_{\mathfrak q}/\mathfrak q^nC_{\mathfrak q}$ compatibly in $n$. Passing to inverse limits proves the assertion. AC is inherited from the completion suppliers; no new choice is used.''','design_row':'AG-BIR-1','local_addition':True,'provenance':{'statement':'literature-derived','proof':'ai-altered'},'sources':{'references':[ref]},'dependency_level':0}
h2={'id':'lem-proper-surface-regularity-transfers-to-and-from-completion','kind':'lemma','title':'Regularity of a proper scheme transfers to and from local-base completion','deps':['def-axiom-of-choice','def-proper-morphism','lem-ag-flat-local-regularity-ascent-descent','lem-proper-stable-base-change','thm-completion-of-a-noetherian-local-ring','thm-completion-preserves-regular-local-rings','cor-localisations-of-regular-local-rings-are-regular',h1['id']],'statement':r'''Assume AC. Let $(A,\mathfrak m)$ be a Noetherian local ring and $X\to\operatorname{Spec}A$ locally of finite type. Set $Y=X\times_A\widehat A$. For $y\in Y$ with image $x\in X$, regularity of $\mathcal O_{Y,y}$ implies regularity of $\mathcal O_{X,x}$. If $y$ lies on the closed fibre, the two local rings are regular simultaneously. If $X$ is proper over $A$, then $X$ is regular if and only if $Y$ is regular. Here regular means that every local ring is regular; no smoothness over a field is asserted.''','strategy':r'''The map $A\to\widehat A$ is faithfully flat by thm-completion-of-a-noetherian-local-ring. Flatness survives base change and localization (tensoring and localizing are exact), so $\mathcal O_{X,x}\to\mathcal O_{Y,y}$ is a flat local map between Noetherian local rings. Descent in lem-ag-flat-local-regularity-ascent-descent gives the first assertion. On the closed fibre the preceding local-completion lemma identifies their completions; thm-completion-preserves-regular-local-rings gives the equivalence. If $X$ is proper, so is $Y$ by lem-proper-stable-base-change, and both are Noetherian. A closed point of either scheme maps to the closed point of its local base because a proper morphism is closed. Every point of a Noetherian scheme has a closed specialization: its closure is Noetherian and has a minimal nonempty closed subset, a closed point since scheme spaces are sober and T0. A local ring at the original point is a localization of the local ring at such a specialization, so cor-localisations-of-regular-local-rings-are-regular shows regularity can be checked at closed points. All these points are on the closed fibres, where the established equivalence applies. AC is inherited from the named suppliers.''','design_row':'AG-BIR-1','local_addition':True,'provenance':{'statement':'literature-derived','proof':'ai-altered'},'sources':{'references':[ref]},'dependency_level':1}
