t['statement']=r'''Assume the Axiom of Choice, inherited from the completion and commutative-algebra suppliers. Let $Y$ be a two-dimensional integral Noetherian scheme. The following are equivalent:
(1) there exists an alteration $X\to Y$ with $X$ regular; (2) there exists a resolution of singularities of $Y$, i.e. a proper birational morphism $\pi\colon X\to Y$ with $X$ regular; (3) there exists a sequence $Y_n\to\cdots\to Y_1\to Y_0\to Y$, proper over $Y$, in which $Y_0\to Y$ is the normalization, every $Y_i\to Y_{i-1}$ is a normalized blowup at a closed point (the blowup followed by its normalization), and $Y_n$ is regular; (4) the normalization $Y^\nu\to Y$ is finite, $Y^\nu$ has finitely many singular points $y_1,\ldots,y_m$, and each $\widehat{\mathcal O_{Y^\nu,y_i}}$ is normal. In particular every integral surface of finite type over any field has a resolution in this form. For a normal surface $Y_0=Y$.

**Step-1 status: escalated.** The arbitrary-Noetherian equivalence is a scaffold strengthening beyond the design's normal-surface-resolution commission; it is preserved pending an owner scope ruling. The complete proof has not been closed locally. The source dependency inventory and exact remaining imports are in the owner-surface-resolution repair report. Neither a necessary count exceeding 100 items nor the necessity of a new pair has been established.'''
t['strategy']=r'''Source route, not a completed local proof: Stacks 54.14.5. The easy implications are (3)⇒(2)⇒(1). For (1)⇒(4), use 54.13.1–3 to prove finite normalization and normal completions; a regular alteration is finite and faithfully flat over an open containing every codimension≤1 point of the normalization, hence that open is regular and its complement is finite. For (4)⇒(3), normalize first. Resolve each complete normal two-dimensional local domain by 54.14.4: induct on the degree of a finite inclusion from a complete regular local ring. Intermediate fields reduce the degree after resolving the intermediate normalization. A separable simple extension is handled by bounded H1 using trace pairing (54.8.6); a purely inseparable degree-p extension uses the differential trace construction and relative duality (54.2, 54.8.8–10). Attain maximal H1 and use its Leray exact sequence to reduce all remaining singularities to rational ones (54.8.1, 54.8.5). Their blowups stay normal (54.9.4), principalize the dualizing module and use its pullback surjectivity to reach Gorenstein rational singularities (54.9.6–8). Resolve these by the complete quadratic-chart case analysis and formal-arc termination (54.10, 54.12, including the unnumbered argument preceding 54.12.3). Lemma 54.14.3 converts a resolution to normalized point blowups, 54.11.6–7 descends their finite normalizations from completion, the local-completion and proper-regularity helpers descend regularity, and 54.6.5 globalizes the local sequences. For finite-type surfaces over a field prove normalization finiteness, openness of the regular locus and normality of completions from excellence; do not substitute the classical-variety normalization theorem or embedded-curve resolution. Open imports include complete-local excellence/Nagata/dualizing existence, relative proper duality and finite-length local duality, domination by admissible blowups, finite-morphism domination and formal gluing. They require actual proofs and source reading before ready; exact source citation edges are recorded, not treated as proof acceptance.'''
